# Extracted from "5AAOB201 Economics terms.pdf" (Useful Economics Definitions).
# Two definitions are reworded slightly so each stands on its own in a question:
# Revaluation ("The opposite:", and it named the term) and Capital liberalisation ("those restrictions").
TERMS = {
    "Money": [
        ("Gold standard", "A system where money is tied to a fixed amount of gold, and paper money can in principle be swapped for gold."),
        ("Gold-exchange standard", "A diluted gold standard where only one currency (the dollar under Bretton Woods) is tied to gold, and other currencies are tied to that currency."),
        ("Fiat currency", "Money that is not backed by gold or anything physical. It has value because the government says it is legal money and people trust it. All major currencies today are fiat."),
        ("Reserve currency", "A currency that other countries hold in large amounts for trade and savings. The US dollar has been the main one since 1945."),
        ("Foreign exchange reserves", "The stock of foreign currencies (and gold) that a central bank keeps, partly so it can buy its own currency to defend its value."),
        ("Exorbitant privilege", "French phrase for America's advantage from the dollar's reserve role: the US can pay for imports and borrow cheaply in its own currency."),
    ],
    "Exchange rates": [
        ("Exchange rate", "The price of one currency in terms of another, e.g. how many dollars one pound buys."),
        ("Fixed exchange rate", "The government sets an official rate and promises to keep it there."),
        ("Pegged currency", "A currency fixed to another currency (or to gold or a basket of currencies). In practice it means much the same as a fixed rate."),
        ("Parity / par value", "The official fixed rate at which a currency is pegged."),
        ("Band (margin of fluctuation)", "The small range around the official rate within which a currency is allowed to move, e.g. plus or minus 2.25 per cent."),
        ("Floating exchange rate", "The rate is set by supply and demand in the markets, not by government."),
        ("Managed float", "A floating rate that the central bank still nudges from time to time."),
        ("Joint float", "A group of currencies fixed to each other but floating together against outside currencies such as the dollar. This is what the Snake became in 1973."),
        ("Devaluation", "A government officially lowers its currency's fixed value. Exports get cheaper, imports dearer."),
        ("Revaluation", "The opposite of devaluation: officially raising a currency's value. Germany was repeatedly pressed to do this to the Deutsche Mark."),
        ("Depreciation / appreciation", "A currency falling or rising in value through market forces rather than a government decision."),
        ("Realignment", "An agreed change to the fixed rates within a system like the EMS, usually some currencies devaluing and others revaluing."),
        ("Anchor currency", "The strongest currency in a group, to which the others effectively have to adjust. In the EMS this was the Deutsche Mark."),
    ],
    "Markets": [
        ("Speculation", "Buying or selling currencies to profit from expected changes in their value."),
        ("Speculative attack", "Investors selling a currency en masse because they expect it to be devalued, which can force the devaluation to happen. Sterling in 1992 is the famous example."),
        ("Intervention", "A central bank buying or selling currencies to push the exchange rate back to where it wants it."),
        ("Capital controls", "Government restrictions on moving money in or out of a country."),
        ("Capital liberalisation / capital mobility", "Removing restrictions on moving money in or out of a country, so money can move freely across borders. Required by the Single European Act, and it made fixed rates much harder to defend."),
        ("Balance of payments", "The record of all money flowing into and out of a country, including trade."),
        ("Trade deficit / surplus", "Importing more than you export (deficit) or exporting more than you import (surplus). Germany ran persistent surpluses."),
    ],
    "Economic conditions and policy": [
        ("Inflation", "A general rise in prices, so money buys less over time."),
        ("Stagflation", "Inflation combined with a stagnant economy and rising unemployment. The defining problem of the 1970s."),
        ("Monetary policy", "Decisions about interest rates and the money supply, made by the central bank."),
        ("Fiscal policy", "Decisions about government spending and taxation, made by governments."),
        ("Interest rate", "The price of borrowing money. Raising rates cools the economy and fights inflation; cutting them stimulates it."),
        ("Central bank", "The institution that issues a country's money and sets interest rates, e.g. the Bundesbank, the Bank of England, the ECB."),
        ("Central bank independence", "The central bank sets policy free from government interference. The Bundesbank was the model, and the ECB copied it."),
        ("Lender of last resort", "An institution that lends money to banks or governments in a crisis when no one else will. The EMS lacked one."),
        ("Austerity", "Cutting public spending and/or raising taxes to reduce deficits."),
        ("Internal devaluation", "Regaining competitiveness by cutting wages and prices at home, because devaluing the currency is not possible. The euro left this as the only option."),
        ("Sound money", "Shorthand for the philosophy of low inflation, balanced budgets and an independent central bank."),
        ("Keynesianism", "The approach of using government spending to boost demand and fight unemployment. Mitterrand tried it in 1981 to 1983 before abandoning it."),
    ],
    "Key systems and policies": [
        ("Bretton Woods system", "The post-1944 monetary order: currencies pegged to the dollar, the dollar convertible to gold at $35 an ounce for governments."),
        ("Triffin dilemma", "The flaw in Bretton Woods: the world needed ever more dollars, but the more dollars there were, the less believable the US promise to swap them for gold."),
        ("Closing the gold window / Nixon shock", "Nixon's August 1971 decision to stop swapping dollars for gold, which effectively ended Bretton Woods."),
        ("Smithsonian Agreement", "The December 1971 attempt to rescue fixed rates with new parities and wider bands. It collapsed by early 1973."),
        ("The Snake", "The European arrangement from 1972 to keep EC currencies close together. It kept losing members and became a Deutsche Mark zone."),
        ("European Monetary System (EMS)", "The 1979 system created by Schmidt and Giscard to stabilise European currencies."),
        ("Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM)", "The core of the EMS: currencies pegged within bands, with realignments possible."),
        ("European Currency Unit (ECU)", "An accounting unit made up of a basket of EC currencies, used as the reference point for the ERM. A forerunner of the euro."),
        ("Franc fort", "The \"strong franc\" policy France adopted after 1983: keeping the franc stable against the Deutsche Mark through low inflation."),
        ("ERM crisis (1992 to 1993)", "The speculative attacks that forced sterling and the lira out of the ERM and led to bands being widened to 15 per cent."),
    ],
    "Concepts from the debates about EMU": [
        ("Economic and Monetary Union (EMU)", "The project of a single European currency managed by a common central bank, alongside economic coordination."),
        ("Monetarists vs economists", "In the EU debate (not the economics school of Milton Friedman): monetarists said fix currencies first and economies will converge; economists said economies must converge first."),
        ("Coronation theory", "The \"economist\" view that a single currency should be the crowning final step after convergence."),
        ("Asymmetry of adjustment", "The burden of keeping currencies aligned falls mainly on weaker-currency countries, not strong ones like Germany."),
        ("External constraint (vincolo esterno)", "Governments using European commitments to justify difficult domestic reforms they couldn't otherwise pass."),
        ("Optimum currency area", "Economic theory on when countries benefit from sharing a currency: e.g. similar economies, mobile workers and shared shocks. Critics said the eurozone was not one."),
        ("Asymmetric shock", "An economic blow that hits some countries in a currency union much harder than others, making one interest rate unsuitable for all."),
        ("Convergence criteria", "The Maastricht entry tests for the euro: low inflation, low interest rates, deficits under 3 per cent of GDP, debt under 60 per cent of GDP, and stable exchange rates."),
        ("Stability and Growth Pact", "The 1997 rules meant to keep deficits and debts within the Maastricht limits after the euro started."),
        ("No-bailout clause", "The Maastricht rule that the EU and other member states are not liable for a member's debts."),
    ],
}

